{{also|Devise}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|devisen|lang=enm}}, {{term|devysen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|deviser|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} ''[[devisare]]'', from {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[divisare]]'', frequentative of ''[[dividere]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-devise.ogg|Audio (US)}}

* {{rhymes|aɪz}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|devis|ing}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To use one's [[intellect]] to [[plan]] or [[design]] (something).
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[leave]] ([[property]]) in a [[will]].
# {{context|intransitive|archaic|lang=en}} To form a scheme; to lay a plan; to contrive; to consider.
#* Alexander Pope
#*: I thought, '''devised''', and Pallas heard my prayer.

